5. Webster's Second International (via /usr/share/dict/words)
--------------------------------------------------------------
The 234,428-word English list embedded at
`crates/mukti-core/data/english-words.fst` is compiled from the word list
shipped with macOS and the BSDs, which is Webster's Second International
(1934). Its copyright has lapsed and it is in the public domain.

Its own README states: "Welcome to web2 (Webster's Second International) all
234,936 words worth. The 1934 copyright has lapsed, according to the supplier."

It is compiled in rather than read from the operating system at runtime,
because Windows has no equivalent file and a guard that silently disappears on
one platform is worse than no guard at all.


6. Rust dependencies
---------------------
Re-audited 19 August 2026, after PDF support and its parser (`lopdf`) were
removed in 0.9.0 along with four other file formats. Two figures now, because
one number was always ambiguous: **79 packages** in the full workspace lock,
including devtools and test-only dependencies, of which **38** are actually
compiled into the shipped `mukti` binary — down from 153 and roughly 88 before
this release, most of that a crypto stack, three date/time stacks and a
work-stealing scheduler that came in only through the PDF parser.

**Re-derive the 79 rather than trusting it.** It is the 87 `[[package]]` entries
in `Cargo.lock` minus this workspace's own 8 members. It read 78 until
`devtools/bench` was added on 20 August 2026 and nothing recomputed it, which is
exactly how a count like this rots: nobody notices a number that is only one out.

Every one
declares a licence, all permissive. No GPL, AGPL or SSPL anywhere.

Direct dependencies of the shipped crates — the complete list, four of them:

  fst                 Unlicense OR MIT   the two embedded dictionaries
                                         (gru953-mukti)
  office_oxide        MIT OR Apache-2.0  reading .doc, .xls and .ppt
  quick-xml           MIT                reading and writing Office XML
  zip                 MIT                Office files are ZIP archives
                                         (the three above: mukti-formats)

`mukti-cli` declares no external dependency of its own. An earlier version of
this file also listed `calamine`; that was wrong — it is not a dependency of this
project and never has been in any commit.

Where a package offers a **choice** of licences, this project takes **MIT**.

**No package in the tree is MPL-2.0, and none is copyleft of any kind.** Five MPL
packages used to be here — `cssparser`, `cssparser-macros`, `dtoa-short`,
`option-ext` and `selectors` — every one part of the web view that drew the
desktop window. They left with it on 15 August 2026. `deny.toml` now allows no
MPL licence at all, so this cannot quietly change back.

**Four packages carry an obligation that electing MIT does not remove**, and they
are named here because a blanket "we choose MIT" would be untrue of them. Each is
in the runtime dependency tree of the shipped binary, verified with
`cargo tree -p mukti-cli --edges normal --invert <package>`:

  encoding_rs    0.8.35   (Apache-2.0 OR MIT) AND BSD-3-Clause
                          The BSD-3-Clause part is AND-ed, so it applies whichever
                          of the other two is chosen. Arrives via office_oxide.
  unicode-ident  1.0.24   (MIT OR Apache-2.0) AND Unicode-3.0
                          Same structure: the Unicode licence applies regardless.
                          Arrives via the proc-macro chains of quick-xml and
                          office_oxide's serde dependency.
  zlib-rs        0.6.7    Zlib
                          No MIT option exists. Arrives via flate2, via zip.
  zopfli         0.8.3    Apache-2.0
                          No MIT option exists. Arrives via zip.

All four are permissive and all four require only that their notice travel with
the software, which is what this file does. None is copyleft.

One further package (`r-efi`) offers "MIT OR Apache-2.0 OR LGPL-2.1-or-later".
The MIT option is taken; no LGPL obligation arises. That is a genuine election,
unlike the four above.

Full licence texts for every dependency can be regenerated at any time with
`cargo license` or `cargo about`. This file, and `LICENSE`, are attached to every
GitHub release beside the binaries, so a person who downloads only a binary still
receives the notices.

7. Fonts, the brand tokens and the GRU953 marks
-----------------------------------------------

**Nothing of the sort ships any more.** Until 15 August 2026 this project also
built a desktop window, which embedded four typefaces under the SIL Open Font
Licence, the brand kit's colour tokens under Apache-2.0, and two GRU953 marks
that were not licensed at all. Each needed its own notice here.

Mukti is now a command-line tool and a library. It embeds no fonts, no colour
tokens and no marks, so those notices have gone with the code they described.

The name GRU953 and the Soaring Bird mark remain the author's and are not
licensed by this project's MIT grant — but neither appears in this repository, so
there is nothing here to carve out.
